<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Solovki prison camp</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Solovki special camp, SLON, Solovki special prison, Solovki</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Solovki prison camp was a Soviet place of detention on the Solovetsky Islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a>, set up in 1923 in the Solovetsky Monastery, at first chiefly for socialist opponents of the Bolshevik regime. One of the first forced labour camps, known by the acronym SLON, it was the nucleus from which the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> grew: from no more than 3,000 prisoners in 1923 it held about 50,000 by 1930, many of them later sent to build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea-baltic-canal">White Sea–Baltic Canal</a>. As a special prison from 1936 it held many who were executed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Terror</a>; all but five of the 1,116 prisoners shipped to the mainland in October 1937 were shot at Sandarmokh. It closed in 1939.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/solovki-prison-camp.jpg" alt="Solovki prison camp" width="330" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:19240000-rail_connection_solovki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Solovetsky Monastery had been a centre of economic life with over three hundred monks and an outpost of Russian naval power in the North; in Tsarist times it was also used on occasion as a political prison. The anti-Bolshevik Provisional Government of the Northern Region used the islands as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> in 1919, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> seized the area in 1920 they held prisoners of the Civil War there. In May 1923 Iosif Unshlikht proposed a forced labour camp for political and criminal prisoners, and the first 300 prisoners arrived on the ship <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechora-river">Pechora</a> on 6 June 1923.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A secret decree of 2 November 1923 turned the monastery into the special camp. At first the anarchists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mensheviks">Mensheviks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialist-revolutionary-party">Socialist Revolutionaries</a> were not made to work, but priests, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Army</a> officers followed, and guards and ordinary criminals together kept the politicals in order. S. A. Malsagoff's memoir An Island Hell, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, drew criticism of the camp in the West, and after careful staging the government sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gorky">Maxim Gorky</a>, who wrote a favourable essay. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naftaly-frenkel">Naftaly Frenkel</a>, a prisoner released in 1927, became head of production at the camp and later of the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>. A mass shooting of 1929 was described by Dmitry Likhachov.</p><p>In February 1938 between 200 and 300 prisoners were shot on the islands near Sekirnaya Hill, as it was too late in the year to cross the frozen sea. Until documents were found in 1996, the transport of October 1937 was long thought to have drowned on deliberately sunk barges. The prison closed because it lay too close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, and its buildings became a naval base.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1989 the museum on the islands opened a permanent exhibition on the special camp, the first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> devoted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>, and the first Days of Remembrance for Victims of Political Repression were held there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> re-established the monastery in 1992, the year the ensemble was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s World Heritage List. In January 2016 the museum's Gulag section was closed by its new director, Vladimir Shutov, head of the monastery as Archimandrite Porfiry; human rights activists had said in 2015 that traces of the camp were being removed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Solovki prison camp” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
